  "sentences": [
    {
      "sid": "S1",
      "index": 1,
      "text": "An object is said to be in motion when its position changes with time."
    },
    {
      "sid": "S2",
      "index": 2,
      "text": "The distance an object covers in a unit of time is called its speed."
    },
    {
      "sid": "S3",
      "index": 3,
      "text": "Speed is calculated by dividing the total distance covered by the total time taken."
    },
    {
      "sid": "S4",
      "index": 4,
      "text": "The basic unit of speed is metre per second (m/s), but speed on roads is usually given in kilometres per hour (km/h)."
    },
    {
      "sid": "S5",
      "index": 5,
      "text": "For example, a bus that covers 120 kilometres in 2 hours has an average speed of 60 km/h."
    },
    {
      "sid": "S6",
      "index": 6,
      "text": "If the time taken stays the same, a vehicle that covers a greater distance must have a higher speed."
    },
    {
      "sid": "S7",
      "index": 7,
      "text": "If two vehicles cover the same distance, the one that takes less time is faster."
    },
    {
      "sid": "S8",
      "index": 8,
      "text": "Most vehicles do not move at a constant speed; they speed up and slow down along the way."
    },
    {
      "sid": "S9",
      "index": 9,
      "text": "That is why we usually talk about average speed."
    },
    {
      "sid": "S10",
      "index": 10,
      "text": "A speedometer in a vehicle shows its speed at that moment, while an odometer measures the total distance it has travelled."
    },
    {
      "sid": "S11",
      "index": 11,
      "text": "Knowing speed helps us plan journeys and estimate how long they will take."
    }
  ],
